 "plan": {
  "angle": "There is no ranked top 10 for this query, and the AlsoAsked pull returned only tipping and etiquette questions. The page wins by treating the massage booking form as a scheduling tool rather than a contact form: it offers only times when a therapist, a room or the drive actually allows a session, it holds a cancellation policy the client agrees to, and it keeps the waitlist and package counts in one table. Health and consent questions belong to the massage intake page, so this page stays on the booking itself.",
  "tab_stories": [
   "Bluecreek Mobile Massage owner Zuri Vandemark keeps 45 minutes of driving between home visits, so when Romilly Prowse in Easton Ridge books a 90-minute visit at 1 p.m., the next opening the form offers is 3:15 p.m.",
   "Osprey Lane Bodywork has three therapists and two rooms; on Tuesday at 5:30 p.m. Kasimir Dunleavy and Solveig Ravel hold both rooms, so the form does not offer 5:30 even though Isolde Quint is free, and Hesper Doane picks first available and gets Isolde at 6:30 p.m.",
   "Thimbleberry Massage asks for 24 hours of notice; a client cancels a Friday 4 p.m. session at 8 p.m. Thursday, 20 hours ahead, the booking is marked a late cancellation, and Anwen Feeney, first of 3 on the waitlist, gets an email with 2 hours to claim the slot.",
   "Daria Ashbury bought a 5-visit package at Hollbrook Massage Clinic; when she books visit 4, the package shows 1 visit left and the app emails her that one visit remains."
  ],
  "openers": [
   "Zuri Vandemark drives to every session she books",
